 \contentsline {chapter}{Figure}{\textbf {Page}}{}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Comparison of no drift versus drift scenarios. Left: When distributions overlap, the discriminative classifier cannot distinguish data sources (AUC $\approx $ 0.5). Right: When drift occurs, distributions shift and the classifier can distinguish sources (AUC $>>$ 0.5).}}{6}{figure.caption.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces High-level overview of the EADD methodology, illustrating the four core conceptual stages.}}{27}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Detailed architecture of the Enhanced Adversarial Drift Detection (EADD) methodology. The framework highlights three novel contributions (blue boxes): (1) Prediction Entropy monitoring ($H_{\text {pred}}$) and Adaptive Sequential Permutation Testing (ASPT) for statistically rigorous drift confirmation with early-warning signals; (2) SHAP Interaction-Aware Localisation and Drift Severity Index (DSI) for feature-level diagnosis and severity quantification; and (3) comprehensive experimental evaluation. This pipeline transforms standard discriminative detection into an actionable MLOps monitoring system.}}{28}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Detailed flowchart of the EADD methodology with mathematical formalisms. The process flows from data windowing (1), through discriminative classification (2) and performance measurement (3), to statistical validation via ASPT. If drift is significant, the framework proceeds to explainable diagnosis with SHAP and severity scoring with DSI, culminating in an actionable MLOps report and prescription. Each novel contribution is shown with its core mathematical representation.}}{29}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Heatmaps of detection performance on synthetic drift. Left: Mean Time to Detection (lower is better; white cells indicate complete miss). Right: False Alarm count (lower is better).}}{51}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Bell curves showing feature distributions before (blue) and after (red) drift injection. Left: Abrupt drift with clear separation. Right: Gradual drift with overlapping distributions---the subtle shift that D3, SPLL, and UDetect all failed to detect.}}{51}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Time-series comparison of all eight detectors. Left: Abrupt drift---most detectors respond near the true drift point (dashed line). Right: Gradual drift---only EADD, BNDM, CSDDM, IBDD, and OCDD detect the shift; D3, SPLL, and UDetect remain silent.}}{51}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Heatmaps of real-world benchmark performance. Left: Mean Time to Detection (lower is better; white cells indicate missed drifts or timeout). Right: Missed Detection Rate (0.0 is perfect).}}{54}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Time-series comparison on real-world datasets. Left: WaveformDrift2---D3 and several baselines detect the annotated drift points, while EADD remains below detection threshold in the current configuration. Right: SineClusters---IBDD detects rapidly but with many excess detections, illustrating the sensitivity--specificity trade-off.}}{54}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Feature distribution bell curves for real-world datasets. Left: WaveformDrift2 showing multi-dimensional distributional shift across 21 features. Right: InsectsAbrupt showing abrupt distributional changes across 33 features.}}{55}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Combined bell curve and SHAP attribution analysis for three controlled scenarios. Left: Univariate drift---F3 dominates at 52.3\%. Center: Subset drift---F5, F2, F7 collectively account for 65\%. Right: Multivariate drift---importance distributed evenly, max 14.6\%.}}{58}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Left: SHAP attribution accuracy across all three scenarios (P@k = R@k = F1@k = 1.0 for all). Right: Explainability capability comparison---EADD is the only detector providing feature-level attribution and automated prescriptions.}}{58}{figure.caption.21}%
